{
  // ts-jest config for tests-main/ (see jest.config.js). Kept separate from
  // tsconfig.json / tsconfig.tests.json — both of those reach code that pulls
  // in @types/jasmine, whose `Matchers<T>` shadows jest's and breaks
  // matchers like `toHaveLength` (same issue worked around in
  // noodl-viewer-cloud/tsconfig.tests.json).
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": ["node", "jest"],
    "noEmit": true,
    "module": "CommonJS"
  },
  // `utils/provenance` is renderer-side but import-free by design (OBS-002); it is
  // reached from tests-unit/ and pulls none of the renderer in with it.
  // WFA-009 adds the second of these: `dynamicPortRules` is renderer-side and
  // import-free by the same design, so `tests-unit/` can grade the port rules
  // (and the committed `cloud-node-library.json` they are declared in) without
  // starting Electron. Its one consumer, `nodelibrary.ts`, is not reached.
  //
  // `utils/import-engine/legacy` is the same shape (LIB-006): the assessment core,
  // the report builder and the verdict are pure, and the two files that are NOT —
  // `catalogQuery.ts` (loads the bundled catalog) and `codePatterns.ts` (pulls
  // ProjectScanner, and through it @noodl/platform) — are reached only by the
  // import shell, never by a test here. Keeping that split is what lets these run
  // without Electron.
  "include": [
    // LGC-008: the ambient declarations the editor's own tsconfig includes — `TSFixme`,
    // `NodeColor` and the `*.scss` / `*.svg` module shims. Everything under `src/editor` that
    // this runner reaches is written against them, and a single `TSFixme` in an otherwise pure
    // file fails the whole suite *to run* rather than failing a spec.
    "@include-types",
    "src/main",
    "tests-main",
    "tests-unit",
    "src/editor/src/utils/provenance",
    "src/editor/src/models/nodelibrary/dynamicPortRules.ts",
    // SPR-003 §1: `portConnectivity.ts` is the third of these — the shared
    // "is this port on the node, and may it be wired" predicate, import-free so
    // the runner can grade it against the real runtime port declarations
    // (which are themselves type-only-import modules) without Electron.
    "src/editor/src/models/nodelibrary/portConnectivity.ts",
    // SPR-003 §4: `docsPopupPlacement.ts` is the same shape once more — the port
    // explainer's flip-and-clamp arithmetic, lifted out of the React component
    // so the edge cases are graded here instead of only by hovering the right
    // port at the right window width.
    "src/editor/src/views/ConnectionPopup/docsPopupPlacement.ts",
    // SIG-001/002/004: three more of the same shape, all in the same folder. The
    // connection popup's *sentences* (`portCopy`), the ranking that picks the
    // wire a builder meant when the editor refused theirs (`refusalPlan`), and
    // the search term that means "where is the Set?" (`searchIntent`). None of
    // the three imports anything — `refusalPlan` deliberately takes the port
    // rows and the already-resolved type names as data rather than reaching for
    // `NodeLibrary` — which is what lets the copy itself be graded here,
    // including the one clause phase 60 forbids.
    "src/editor/src/views/ConnectionPopup/portCopy.ts",
    "src/editor/src/views/ConnectionPopup/refusalPlan.ts",
    "src/editor/src/views/ConnectionPopup/searchIntent.ts",
    "src/editor/src/models/nodelibrary/cloud-node-library.json",
    "src/editor/src/utils/import-engine/legacy",
    "src/editor/src/validation",
    // MCP-001: `mcpCommands.ts` is the same shape again — the settings section's command builder
    // imports nothing at all, which is what lets the plain-Node runner assert the exact strings
    // the two Copy buttons put on the clipboard. Its component neighbour is not reached.
    "src/editor/src/views/panels/SettingsPanel/sections/mcpCommands.ts",
    // ALPHA-006 §1: `utils/nodeDocs.ts` is the same shape again — it renders the
    // bundled enriched catalog into help HTML and imports nothing but
    // `validation/enrichedCatalog` (already here). The docs *origin* is
    // deliberately resolved by its renderer call sites rather than inside it,
    // which is exactly what keeps `@electron/remote` out of this runner.
    "src/editor/src/utils/nodeDocs.ts",
    // BLD-001: `AiAssistant/thread/` is the same shape once more — the turn model that
    // maps the three session states onto one thread. Every edge it has to `../authoring`
    // and `../review` is an `import type`, so nothing of the renderer (or of
    // `AuthoringSession`, which reaches ProjectModel) survives compilation. That is what
    // lets the 37 specs in `tests-unit/bld-001/` grade the mapping without Electron —
    // and it is a boundary worth keeping: a single value import here moves these specs
    // into the jasmine runner.
    "src/editor/src/models/AiAssistant/thread",
    // SIG-005: `wirePulse.ts` is the same shape again — the travelling mark's
    // arithmetic (which mark a wire gets, where its head is at a given age, how
    // far the value overlay has drifted) lifted out of the canvas painter so it
    // is graded here rather than only by running an app and watching a wire.
    // Import-free by design: it takes the point function as an argument rather
    // than reaching for the connection, which is also what lets SIG-006's hover
    // reuse it without inheriting the painter.
    "src/editor/src/views/nodegrapheditor/wirePulse.ts",
    // SIG-006: `wireEndpoints.ts` is its neighbour and the same shape — the
    // endpoint glyph geometry (which shape a plug gets, the arrowhead's three
    // points, the diamond's four, and how much to inflate them so they hold
    // their screen size as the graph zooms out). Import-free for the same
    // reason: the fill-ratio separation these shapes are chosen for is a
    // property of the polygons, so it can be graded as arithmetic here and
    // confirmed once on composited pixels, rather than argued about.
    "src/editor/src/views/nodegrapheditor/wireEndpoints.ts",
    // FUN-002: `newNodeSeed.ts` is the same shape again — whether a just-created
    // node gets a body, and which parameter it goes in. Import-free on purpose:
    // the seed *string* is FUN-001's and is passed in by the two call sites
    // rather than imported here, which is what keeps `@noodl-core-ui` (and
    // through it React and CodeMirror) out of this runner. The two guards it
    // holds — wrong node type, node already has a body — both fail silently in
    // the app, so they are graded here rather than by remembering to try them.
    "src/editor/src/models/nodeSeed/newNodeSeed.ts",
    // LGC-001 §2: `NodePicker.chooser.ts` is the same shape once more — the three
    // sentences that say which of Expression / Visual Function / Function to
    // reach for, lifted out of the preview component so the *copy* is graded
    // here rather than only by cursoring a row and reading it. Import-free by
    // design, and deliberately not part of the enriched catalog: this is a
    // comparative note about three neighbours, and the catalog is written one
    // node at a time. The clause it must never contain — that the Function node
    // has one signal in and one out, which is false in both directions — is the
    // reason it is graded at all.
    "src/editor/src/views/NodePicker/NodePicker.chooser.ts",
    // LGC-008: `blocklyResize.ts` is the same shape once more — the registry a
    // splitter drag calls to make Blockly re-measure itself against its
    // container. It is import-free *by requirement* rather than by luck: Blockly
    // is a ~1.1 MB lazy chunk and the layout code that triggers a resize sits in
    // the eager bundle, so the module joining them must never name `blockly`.
    // That constraint is exactly what lets the dispatch behaviour be graded here
    // with no Electron, no DOM and no Blockly.
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/blocklyResize.ts",
    // LGC-007: `views/BlocklyEditor/myblocks/` is the same shape again, and here
    // the boundary is enforced by where a file sits rather than by remembering.
    // The whole directory is pure — the saved-definition format, the cycle
    // guard, the shape inference and the inliner — and imports nothing but
    // itself, not even Blockly: a saved group is plain JSON, so all four are
    // JSON transforms. Anything that touches Blockly or an editor singleton (the
    // two shelf adapters, the call-block definitions) lives one level up in
    // `BlocklyEditor/` and is deliberately not reached from here.
    //
    // That split matters more than usual for this feature. §3's failure mode is
    // not an error message, it is a hung renderer inside a 300 ms debounce tick,
    // which presents as the workspace freezing at random — the one class of
    // defect a drive is worst at finding and a plain-Node runner is best at.
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/myblocks",
    // …and these three, which are not pure but are reachable anyway: all each
    // one imports is `blockly` / `blockly/javascript`, and Blockly runs headless
    // in Node. That is what lets `inliner.test.ts` close the loop the rest of
    // this feature cannot close by inspection — expand the JSON, load it into a
    // real headless workspace, run the **real** generators, and assert the
    // JavaScript that comes out. Without them the inliner could only be graded
    // on the shape of the JSON it produces, which is precisely the kind of
    // proof that passes while the generated program does nothing.
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/initialize.ts",
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/NoodlBlocks.ts",
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/NoodlGenerators.ts",
    // LGC-005: the Blockly type map and the block definitions that apply it. The map itself
    // (`NoodlTypes.ts`) imports nothing at all; `NoodlBlocks.ts` imports only `blockly`, which
    // runs headlessly in plain Node as long as nothing asks for a `WorkspaceSvg` — so the
    // specs drive a real workspace and grade Blockly's own refusals rather than a
    // re-implementation of them. Nothing else in `views/BlocklyEditor/` is reachable from
    // here: `BlocklyWorkspace.tsx` is React and `BlocklyTheme.ts` reaches the canvas theme.
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/NoodlTypes.ts",
    // LGC-002: the same shape a third time. `DoIt.ts` is the offer rule ("is this a value
    // block, and if not, what does the greyed menu item say?") plus the fragment generator, and
    // it imports nothing but `blockly/javascript` — so both are graded against a real headless
    // workspace, with the real blocks and the real generator. `NoodlGenerators.ts` comes with
    // it because the fragment for a `get input` block *is* its output; grading generation
    // against anything else would be grading a mock. The three files deliberately NOT reachable
    // from here are the three that must not be: `DoItController` (Blockly's rendered menu),
    // `DoItBalloons` (SVG and the canvas theme) and `DoItProbeClient` (the socket).
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/DoIt.ts",
    // LGC-003: the same shape a fourth time, and here the split is the whole design.
    // `BlockProbes.ts` is the instrumentation — it decides what `__p` wraps and at what
    // operator precedence — and it imports nothing but Blockly, so the claim the acceptance
    // list actually cares about ("instrumented and uninstrumented runs of the same program
    // produce identical outputs") is proved by *running both* against a real workspace with the
    // real generator rather than by reading the emitted string. `BlockValueTrace.ts` imports
    // nothing at all: it is the didn't-execute rule, the loop count, the run history and the
    // frame coalescing — every decision §2, §3 and §5 make. Deliberately NOT reachable:
    // `BlockValueBadges` (SVG + canvas theme), `BlockTraceClient` (the socket) and
    // `BlockValueController` (both, plus the DOM).
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/BlockProbes.ts",
    "src/editor/src/views/BlocklyEditor/BlockValueTrace.ts"
  ]
}
